A||||10||fotofrank||ralley Gauges in an '84 SE||||5||
Z||000000||fotofrank||02-28-2000||08:00 PM||||I recently installed a set of ralley gauges in my '84 SE. Here is the problem. 1) or A), I do not have a wire on C3 pin 17. 2) or B), with every wire from the gauge pod to C3 as outlinded by Batman and the Big Fiero book with the exception of the tan wier to C3 pin 17 the thing doesn't work right. The oil pressure is peged to the left and the amp meter is also peged. When I turn on the lights the oil pressuer needle moves to about mid scale and the amp meter dips to about 1/4 scale and the light does not come on in the gauge pod. With the wire for the pod lights disconnected the ampmeter is OK and of cource (no tan wire on pin 17) the oil pressure is piged. What now? I have a drawing from the popular mechanics repair CD and the call out of the C3 pins is not what Batman and the Big Fiero book say.It seems not all '84 Fieros and equal.||||reg||5
Z||000001||daved||02-28-2000||09:48 PM||||Foto, you are right in that 84's seem to have slightly different wiring to later models. That said, how can you make the gauges work?<BR>The Voltmeter (you refered to as ammeter?) is easy! The "A" [pnk/blk]connection is any hot in run +12 volts, run it right from the 'gage fuse' (also goes to the supply end of the press. gauge).  The "H" [blk]connection goes to any ground (also goes to the ground end of the press. gauge).<BR>Now the Volts will read correctly.<BR>I would leave the 'battery charge' indicator on the main pod.<BR>That leaves the "B" [tan]connector from the center connection on the press. gauge. You will have to trace this wire [tan] from the oil pressure sensor on the engine, sorry.<BR>If you have engine code VIN R, then it will already be at the pod. Maybe your car never had a press. gauge on the pod, so no wire was run.  And more to the point, perhaps not even the oil press. sensor would have been installed.<BR>Anyone know this?<BR>See Haynes manual, bottom of page 288.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-28-2000||10:16 PM||||I've never had a 84, doesn't it have a oil pressure gauge built into the tach or no? I do remember reading that you have to replace the 84 oil pressure sending unit with a newer one when you add the ralley gauges. I wonder if this has anything to do with it? Sorry, I wish the 84 wasn't such an oddball. Those instructions I wrote up worked good for my 85 Batmobile.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||02-29-2000||06:01 AM||||I was under the impression that the '84 had a <B>volt</B> gauge under the tach like in this pic, but I could be wrong.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/inst_cluster_with_volts.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||02-29-2000||08:54 AM||||Some of the non-rally pods had volt gauges under the tach, others had oil psi gauges.  However, I am thinking that the oil gauges showed up in 85, but I dont know for sure.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||cscougar||02-29-2000||03:12 PM||||I have installed an auxiliary guage pod and 120 mph speedo assembly in my 85 2m4.  Everything works fine except the oil presssure guage is pegging all the way to the right.  I spliced into the tan wire at the C3 connector and am wondering if I should clip that wire so that no signal goes to the instrument pod and the signal goes only to the guage pod for the oil pressure.<P>Any ideas anyone?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000006||cscougar||02-29-2000||03:17 PM||||I recently installed a 120 mph speedo cluster and auxiliary guage pod in my 85 2m4.  Everything works fine but the oil pressure guage is pegged to the right.  I connected to the tan wire in the wiring harness as instructed but am wondering since the instrument cluster has no oil pressure guage, do I need to clip the tan wire so that the signal only goes to the oil pressure guage.<P>Anyone with any ideas on this?||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-29-2000||03:40 PM||||I had the same problem when I tried to run both oil pressure gauges. You can only use one or the other. The tach that matches the 120 mph speedo does not have an oil pressure gauge built into it. That's the one you're "supposed" to use with the ralley gauge pod. I have since replaced the old tach, so my oil pressure gauge in the ralley pod works fine.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||02-29-2000||04:27 PM||||Monkeyman is right!  The 84's do not have an oil pressure gauge, just a light.  They have a voltmeter at the bottom of the tach, and water temp and fuel gauge in the middle.||||reg||10
Z||000009||ggarsk||02-29-2000||07:33 PM||||According to the GM shop manual wiring diagram,the volt meter and the oil light run off the same circuit. I still have yet to figure a way around this. I know one thing Fiero's are a constant work in progress.||||reg||1
Z||000010||cscougar||03-01-2000||08:43 PM||||Well, I got the rally guages working right.  It was the tan wire was loose.  I thought I had it connected at the C3 connector but i must have pulled loose.  Funny thing.  When I hooked up the 120 mph speedometer cluster, I now have fuel guage.  The old one never worked and I thought it was the sending unit in the tank.  <P>Thanks for all the help and that wiring diagram really was a life saver.||||reg||1
